# config: mock-wedge.json
#
# An engine killed for not answering must be put back.
#
# The shape is GNU Go's: `final_status_list dead` on a sparse board never
# returns, so GtpClient times out and kills the process — correctly, because a
# late reply read as the answer to the *next* command is silently wrong results.
# What was missing was the counterpart. terminateProcess() had no undo, so one
# double pass on a thin board removed the coach for the rest of the session, and
# the only trace was a log line: every later move went to no engine at all.
#
# GameThread::reviveFailedEngines() now respawns it between loop iterations —
# the only thread allowed to touch those pipes — and marks every engine
# Unsynced, so the record is replayed into the replacement.

new_game 9
wait_idle
switch_player black human
switch_player white human
wait_idle

# Two passes end the game, which is what asks the coach to count.
pass
wait_until move_count >= 1
wait_idle
pass
wait_until phase finished

# The coach never answers, is killed after scoring_timeout_ms, and the position
# is reported honestly rather than as a score of zero.
wait_until msg scoring_failed

# ...and the engine comes back. Unsynced is the visible consequence: the
# replacement holds an empty board, so the loop replays the record into it.
wait_until engine_sync synced
wait_idle

# The proof that it is really back: navigating and playing both need a live
# coach, and both go through it. Before the revive these were silently refused.
navigate_back
wait_idle
expect view_position 1

# White is to move here: move 2 was White's pass, so undoing it leaves White on
# turn. The stone lands as a variation on the second pass.
click 3 3
wait_idle
expect holds_stone true
click 3 3
wait_until white_stones 1
wait_idle
expect white_stones 1
expect view_position 2
